29:<p>The initial buyer is the managing director or service director of a Benelux rotating-equipment maintenance company. The business runs scheduled vibration, temperature, oil or electrical routes for several factories, but lacks a credible customer portal and analytics team. The purchase event is a contract renewal or competitive tender. Its industrial customer asks for continuous monitoring, consolidated asset history or evidence that route measurements cause action. The partner fears losing the account to a national technical-services group or an equipment vendor with a digital offer.</p>
33:<p>The substitute is not a clipboard. The partner can keep reporting in Excel and PDFs, deploy a customer's incumbent EAM/APM suite, resell an equipment vendor's portal, or hire one analyst and assemble dashboards from commodity telemetry tools. The proposed layer wins only if it makes those routes and reports cheaper to operate while preserving the partner's brand. “Better AI” is neither the opening claim nor a defensible boundary.</p>
38:<p>The proposition does not rely on a legal mandate. The change is that the pieces have become easier to connect while the complete offer has become harder to differentiate. Siemens describes Senseye machine data as data used for predictive-maintenance purposes. It documents ingestion through API, MQTT, email, S3, Azure Blob Storage or directly from a historian. Its ordinary time-series interface accepts up to 1 Hz; it also accepts vibration waveforms and spectra. <!-- CHECK: 04-senseye-ingestion --> A small entrant no longer needs to invent transport or charting. It does need to decide what every source means and whether two names describe the same motor.</p>
42:<p>That distinction separates obligation from budget. This proposition does not depend on a statutory deadline or public procurement demand. Vendor breadth shows a real job; it does not show willingness to pay this entrant. The budget must be discovered inside a live renewal. Ask what revenue is at risk, what analyst hours are consumed, and whether a branded evidence trail changes the partner's win rate or service margin. Until a partner pays, demand remains a hypothesis.</p>
48:<p>The account list should include firms such as VIBRO and Istec as tests of the specialist end, not as assertions that they need the product. The opening is not “we built predictive AI.” It is: “Give us one redacted route export, one asset list and twenty closed work orders. In four weeks we will show whether your reports can become a repeatable branded service.” A warm introduction from a reliability engineer, sensor distributor or CMMS implementer is more credible than English-language outbound to a Dutch service director. The founder's A2 Dutch is adequate for technical preparation, not for discovering ambiguity in a plant-floor failure account. The partner must lead those conversations; an independent Dutch-speaking reliability specialist must join the first deliveries.</p>
60:<tr><td><strong>Year 1:</strong> four diagnostics; two paying-partner onboardings; four sites and 500 assets live for an average six months.</td><td>Diagnostics €30k; onboarding €36k; site fees €48k; asset fees €36k. <strong>Total €150k.</strong></td><td>Contract analyst €32k; telemetry/cloud €12k. <strong>Gross margin €106k (71%).</strong></td><td>Founder delivery at €75k replacement cost plus €35k legal, insurance, security, travel and sales leaves <strong>–€4k</strong>. No distributable owner income.</td></tr>
61:<tr><td><strong>Year 3 design:</strong> five paying partners; twelve monitored sites; average 160 assets per site; four new partner onboardings in the year.</td><td>Site fees €288k; asset fees €276k; onboarding €72k. <strong>Total €636k.</strong></td><td>Analyst review €84k; hosting/telemetry €42k. <strong>Gross margin €510k (80%).</strong></td><td>Delivery engineer €82k; partner success/technical writing €60k; founder replacement salary €100k; sales/travel €30k; insurance, legal and security €50k. <strong>€188k remains</strong> before corporation tax and financing.</td></tr>
63:<p>In the year-three design, the company can pay the target owner compensation and retain meaningful cash. It also reveals how little room there is for channel discounting. Cut recurring prices by 25% while direct review costs stay fixed and revenue falls by €141,000; most retained margin disappears. The partner may add its own margin, but the software company cannot behave like a wholesale licence with a token fee. Its share must pay for analyst review, integration variance, professional liability and support.</p>
68:<tr><td><strong>Year 1</strong></td><td>Two partners, four plants, four sites and 500 active assets; two sites per partner.</td><td>Two production onboardings; €36k onboarding and €84k recurring revenue.</td><td>Founder plus contracted analyst; €75k founder replacement cost; no churn assumed; €4k operating loss.</td></tr>
69:<tr><td><strong>Year 3</strong></td><td>Five partners, twelve plants, twelve sites and 1,920 assets; 2.4 sites per partner.</td><td>Four gross partner onboardings; €72k onboarding and €564k recurring revenue.</td><td>Founder, one analyst, one delivery engineer and one partner-success role. €100k owner compensation and €188k retained before tax. No partner may exceed three sites or 25% of recurring revenue. The hurdle allows at most one partner loss in 24 months and one site loss a year.</td></tr>
70:<tr><td><strong>Year 5</strong></td><td>Twenty partners, forty plants, forty sites and 6,400 assets; two sites per partner.</td><td>Eight gross partner onboardings in the year; €144k onboarding and a €1.882m recurring-revenue run rate.</td><td>Founder replacement, three analysts, three delivery engineers and two partner-success roles. Owner compensation remains €100k; retained cash must exceed €300k before tax after the added staff and overhead. Annual partner churn must stay below 10%, and no partner may exceed 10% of recurring revenue.</td></tr>
72:<p>Gross margin is protected by refusing pass-through hardware. Sensors, gateways, installation and replacement are specified and invoiced by the maintenance partner. Putting them through this company's books would inflate revenue, create warranty exposure and consume working capital without creating software margin. Annual subscriptions should be billed quarterly in advance, with onboarding 50% in advance and 50% on acceptance. Even then, the year-one company needs roughly €60,000–€80,000 for a six-month founder gap, legal drafting, insurance, a contracted analyst and security work. Keep at least two months of year-three payroll and contractor cost—about €54,000 under this design—rather than treating all cash as owner income.</p>
80:<p><strong>Paying partner twenty</strong>, not site twenty, is the scalability gate because the partner signs and can churn. It should bring the portfolio to about forty plants and forty sites. Partners six through twenty must arrive through a documented referral programme for sensor distributors, reliability specialists and CMMS implementers. The twentieth must have no prior founder relationship. It receives a standard data template, demonstration, diagnostic offer and production contract; bespoke founder-led courtship fails the gate.</p>
102:<li><strong>Days 1–15:</strong> interview ten owners or service directors across the ten prospect types. Ask for the last renewal where digital reporting mattered, current report-production hours, the software substitute and who owns raw data and work outcomes. Obtain two redacted route exports and matching closeouts under a simple NDA. Spend no more than €2,500 on travel and Dutch technical support.</li>
109:<p><strong>Pass:</strong> one paying maintenance partner buys the diagnostic and introduces a named end-customer plant. It accepts the liability boundary and data feedback, signs the production order, and pays the €9,000 onboarding deposit. It must then pay the first quarterly recurring invoice at the stated site and asset prices. Alternatively, it must present a binding end-customer-backed commitment fixing the resale price, start date and recurring funding. The partner records at least 20% resale gross margin after incremental delivery labour. That percentage is a test hurdle, not an observed market rate. <strong>Kill:</strong> no paid diagnostic after five properly scoped offers; no partner will return coded work outcomes; or every credible partner blocks a reusable connector and runbook asset. In any kill state, offer integration consulting only if premium self-employment is acceptable. Do not call it the second engine.</p>
