 <section class="proposition">
 <h2>The proposition</h2>
-<p>The initial buyer is the managing director or service director of a Benelux rotating-equipment maintenance company: large enough to run scheduled vibration, temperature, oil or electrical routes for several factories, small enough not to have a credible customer portal and analytics team. The purchase event is a contract renewal or competitive tender in which its industrial customer asks for continuous monitoring, consolidated asset history or evidence that route measurements cause action. The partner fears losing the account to a national technical-services group or an equipment vendor with a digital offer.</p>
-<p>The deliverable is deliberately narrower than “predictive maintenance.” It ingests the partner's route files and selected online streams; reconciles asset identities; preserves measurements, alarms, reviews and work outcomes; gives an analyst a queue; publishes a white-label monthly report; and opens a documented route into the customer's CMMS. A contracted vibration or reliability specialist reviews escalations. The local partner installs sensors, owns the route, speaks Dutch with technicians, recommends and performs repairs, and invoices the plant.</p>
+<p>The initial buyer is the managing director or service director of a Benelux rotating-equipment maintenance company. The business runs scheduled vibration, temperature, oil or electrical routes for several factories, but lacks a credible customer portal and analytics team. The purchase event is a contract renewal or competitive tender. Its industrial customer asks for continuous monitoring, consolidated asset history or evidence that route measurements cause action. The partner fears losing the account to a national technical-services group or an equipment vendor with a digital offer.</p>
+<p>Three units matter. A <strong>paying maintenance partner</strong> signs the wholesale contract. An <strong>end-customer plant</strong> buys maintenance from that partner. A <strong>monitored site</strong> is one billable facility covered by the production subscription. The initial model assumes one monitored site per plant and, after the first deployment, about two sites per paying partner.</p>
+<p>The deliverable is deliberately narrower than “predictive maintenance.” It ingests the partner's route files and selected online streams, reconciles asset identities, and preserves measurements, alarms, reviews and work outcomes. It gives an analyst a queue, publishes a white-label monthly report, and opens a documented route into the plant's CMMS. A contracted vibration or reliability specialist reviews escalations. The local partner installs sensors, owns the route, speaks Dutch with technicians, recommends and performs repairs, and invoices the plant.</p>
 <p>The business refuses five things at the outset: supplying sensor hardware, promising remaining useful life, remotely declaring a machine safe, taking emergency calls from the plant, or selling around the partner. It returns one of three controlled outputs—observe, re-measure, or specialist escalation—with the evidence and confidence attached. A maintenance recommendation is issued only by the named partner analyst. This boundary is not legal decoration. Without it, one missed bearing fault can turn a software subscription into an argument about consequential production loss.</p>
 <p>The substitute is not a clipboard. The partner can keep reporting in Excel and PDFs, deploy a customer's incumbent EAM/APM suite, resell an equipment vendor's portal, or hire one analyst and assemble dashboards from commodity telemetry tools. The proposed layer wins only if it makes those routes and reports cheaper to operate while preserving the partner's brand. “Better AI” is neither the opening claim nor a defensible boundary.</p>
 </section>
 
 <section class="changed">
 <h2>What changed</h2>
-<p>There is no new law forcing this purchase. The change is that the pieces have become easier to connect while the complete offer has become harder to differentiate. Siemens documents Senseye ingestion through API, MQTT, email, S3 or Azure Blob Storage, or directly from a historian. Its ordinary time-series interface accepts up to 1 Hz and requires preprocessing above that rate; it also accepts vibration waveforms and spectra. <!-- CHECK: 04-senseye-ingestion --> A small entrant no longer needs to invent transport or charting. It does need to decide what every source means and whether two names describe the same motor.</p>
+<p>The proposition does not rely on a legal mandate. The change is that the pieces have become easier to connect while the complete offer has become harder to differentiate. Siemens describes Senseye machine data as data used for predictive-maintenance purposes. It documents ingestion through API, MQTT, email, S3, Azure Blob Storage or directly from a historian. Its ordinary time-series interface accepts up to 1 Hz; it also accepts vibration waveforms and spectra. <!-- CHECK: 04-senseye-ingestion --> A small entrant no longer needs to invent transport or charting. It does need to decide what every source means and whether two names describe the same motor.</p>
 <p>The feedback record matters more than the dashboard. Senseye's work-event API requires an asset, timestamp, message, routine/non-routine flag and work-activity type; “failure mode” is optional. Siemens says that a timely completion event lets its system learn how degradation evolved and relate subsequent data changes to the work. <!-- CHECK: 04-senseye-work-events --> That optional field exposes the commercial problem. A service report reading “checked, OK” closes a job for invoicing but does not say whether an alarm was a bearing defect, misalignment, process upset, loose sensor or false positive. Without a disciplined closeout, the dataset grows and the diagnostic asset does not.</p>
 <p>Incumbents already sell the whole loop. Augury describes a subscription combining industrial sensors, AI diagnostics, expert validation and connection into maintenance workflow, and markets coverage across more than 200 asset types. <!-- CHECK: 04-augury-bundle --> SKF describes software connected to CMMS/EAM, collection by the customer, a service partner or SKF, and subscription access to certified remote analysts. <!-- CHECK: 04-skf-bundle --> IBM Maximo APM combines condition data, asset history, failure patterns, reliability workflows and recommended actions inside an enterprise asset suite. <!-- CHECK: 04-maximo-bundle --> These are vendor descriptions, not independent proof of outcomes. They are decisive evidence of product surface area: software, expertise and workflow are not an empty niche.</p>
-<p>The Dutch channel is not waiting to be digitised either. SPIE Nederland presents machine maintenance, asset-management advice, maintenance-system implementation and a partnership with IFS Ultimo on the same service page. <!-- CHECK: 04-spie-offer --> ERIKS has marketed a Smart Asset Management team including software and application engineers and a platform that collects and presents machine data. <!-- CHECK: 04-eriks-offer --> At the specialist end, VIBRO offers condition monitoring, telemonitoring, vibration analysis, maintenance advice and sensor calibration. <!-- CHECK: 04-vibro-offer --> Istec says it combines vibration hardware, certified experts and analysis. <!-- CHECK: 04-istec-offer --> The plausible partner is therefore not “a maintenance firm that lacks software.” It is one whose current tool is too narrow, too expensive, tied to one sensor family, or weak under its own brand.</p>
-<p>That distinction separates obligation from budget. There is no statutory deadline and no public procurement trail here showing small maintenance firms buying a neutral intelligence layer. Vendor breadth shows a real job; it does not show willingness to pay this entrant. The budget must be discovered inside a live renewal: what revenue is at risk, what analyst hours are being consumed, and whether a branded evidence trail changes the partner's win rate or service margin. Until a partner pays, demand remains a hypothesis.</p>
+<p>The Dutch channel is not waiting to be digitised either. SPIE Nederland presents machine maintenance, asset-management advice, maintenance-system implementation and a partnership with IFS Ultimo on the same service page. <!-- CHECK: 04-spie-offer --> ERIKS describes Smart Asset Management using sensors and data analysis to give real-time insight across a machine fleet. <!-- CHECK: 04-eriks-offer --> At the specialist end, VIBRO offers condition monitoring, telemonitoring, vibration analysis, maintenance advice and sensor calibration. <!-- CHECK: 04-vibro-offer --> Istec says it combines vibration hardware, certified experts and analysis. <!-- CHECK: 04-istec-offer --> The plausible partner is therefore not “a maintenance firm that lacks software.” It is one whose current tool is too narrow, too expensive, tied to one sensor family, or weak under its own brand.</p>
+<p>That distinction separates obligation from budget. This proposition does not depend on a statutory deadline or public procurement demand. Vendor breadth shows a real job; it does not show willingness to pay this entrant. The budget must be discovered inside a live renewal. Ask what revenue is at risk, what analyst hours are consumed, and whether a branded evidence trail changes the partner's win rate or service margin. Until a partner pays, demand remains a hypothesis.</p>
 </section>
 
 <section class="sale">
 <h2>The first sale</h2>
-<p>Start with ten prospect types, each close enough to rotating machinery to own the outcome: an independent vibration-analysis practice; pump repairer; motor and gearbox workshop; industrial lubrication contractor; compressor-service firm; marine rotating-equipment specialist; food-factory maintenance contractor; water and wastewater service company; multi-brand HVAC/chiller service firm; and a regional electrical-mechanical maintenance provider. Exclude OEM-only dealers whose economics depend on pulling the customer into their own cloud, and exclude national groups already selling an APM stack.</p>
+<p>Start with ten prospect types close enough to rotating machinery to own the outcome. Approach independent vibration practices, pump repairers, motor and gearbox workshops, lubrication contractors, and compressor-service firms. Add marine specialists, food-factory contractors, water-service companies, HVAC/chiller firms, and regional electrical-mechanical providers. Exclude OEM-only dealers whose economics depend on pulling the customer into their own cloud. Also exclude national groups already selling an APM stack.</p>
 <p>The account list should include firms such as VIBRO and Istec as tests of the specialist end, not as assertions that they need the product. The opening is not “we built predictive AI.” It is: “Give us one redacted route export, one asset list and twenty closed work orders. In four weeks we will show whether your reports can become a repeatable branded service.” A warm introduction from a reliability engineer, sensor distributor or CMMS implementer is more credible than English-language outbound to a Dutch service director. The founder's A2 Dutch is adequate for technical preparation, not for discovering ambiguity in a plant-floor failure account. The partner must lead those conversations; an independent Dutch-speaking reliability specialist must join the first deliveries.</p>
 <p>The first product is a four-week, fixed-scope <strong>€7,500 paid data-and-service diagnostic</strong>. This is a proposed test price, not an observed market rate. It covers up to forty assets and three input formats: identity reconciliation; a data-quality and connectivity map; a failure/closeout taxonomy; two sample customer reports; an escalation runbook; and a proposed data-rights schedule. It does not include production connectors, model training or hardware. Half is payable before work. Free pilots select for curiosity and leave the core question—whether the partner can resell the layer—untested.</p>
-<p>The service director signs, but the diagnostic does not begin until a named end customer approves use of its data and the partner nominates both a route technician and someone who closes work orders. The production offer is quoted at a required-price hurdle: €18,000 onboarding, then €2,000 per monitored site per month plus €12 per active asset per month, with a 100-asset minimum. Hardware and field labour remain on the partner's invoice. The partner chooses its markup. If it cannot place that price inside a maintenance contract with a measurable gross-margin improvement or retention benefit, a cheaper portal will not fix the problem.</p>
+<p>The service director signs, but the diagnostic does not begin until a named end-customer plant approves use of its data. The partner must also nominate a route technician and someone who closes work orders. The production offer uses a required-price hurdle: €18,000 to onboard a paying partner's first site. Recurring wholesale charges are €2,000 per monitored site each month plus €12 per active asset each month, with a 100-asset minimum.</p>
+<p>Hardware and field labour remain on the partner's invoice. The production order must record the plant-facing resale price, the partner's incremental delivery labour, and its expected resale gross margin. If it cannot create positive margin without hiding a subsidy elsewhere in the maintenance contract, a cheaper portal will not fix the problem.</p>
 </section>
 
 <section class="economics">
 <h2>Does the money work?</h2>
 <p>The arithmetic below is a design gate. None of the prices, conversion rates or costs is reported as a market average. They state what must become true.</p>
-<div class="table-wrap"><table>
+<div class="ledger-wrap"><table class="ledger">
 <thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Revenue</th><th>Direct cost / gross margin</th><th>Operating result</th></tr></thead>
 <tbody>
-<tr><td><strong>Year 1:</strong> four diagnostics; two production onboardings; four sites and 500 assets live for an average six months.</td><td>Diagnostics €30k; onboarding €36k; site fees €48k; asset fees €36k. <strong>Total €150k.</strong></td><td>Contract analyst €32k; telemetry/cloud €12k. <strong>Gross margin €106k (71%).</strong></td><td>Founder delivery at €75k replacement cost plus €35k legal, insurance, security, travel and sales leaves <strong>–€4k</strong>. No distributable owner income.</td></tr>
-<tr><td><strong>Mature test:</strong> twelve sites, average 160 assets; four new onboardings in the year.</td><td>Site fees €288k; asset fees €276k; onboarding €72k. <strong>Total €636k.</strong></td><td>Analyst review €84k; hosting/telemetry €42k. <strong>Gross margin €510k (80%).</strong></td><td>Delivery engineer €82k; partner success/technical writing €60k; founder replacement salary €100k; sales/travel €30k; insurance, legal and security €50k. <strong>€188k remains</strong> before corporation tax and financing.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><strong>Year 1:</strong> four diagnostics; two paying-partner onboardings; four sites and 500 assets live for an average six months.</td><td>Diagnostics €30k; onboarding €36k; site fees €48k; asset fees €36k. <strong>Total €150k.</strong></td><td>Contract analyst €32k; telemetry/cloud €12k. <strong>Gross margin €106k (71%).</strong></td><td>Founder delivery at €75k replacement cost plus €35k legal, insurance, security, travel and sales leaves <strong>–€4k</strong>. No distributable owner income.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><strong>Year 3 design:</strong> five paying partners; twelve monitored sites; average 160 assets per site; four new partner onboardings in the year.</td><td>Site fees €288k; asset fees €276k; onboarding €72k. <strong>Total €636k.</strong></td><td>Analyst review €84k; hosting/telemetry €42k. <strong>Gross margin €510k (80%).</strong></td><td>Delivery engineer €82k; partner success/technical writing €60k; founder replacement salary €100k; sales/travel €30k; insurance, legal and security €50k. <strong>€188k remains</strong> before corporation tax and financing.</td></tr>
 </tbody></table></div>
-<p>In the mature case the company can pay the target owner compensation and retain meaningful cash. It also reveals how little room there is for channel discounting. Cut recurring prices by 25% while direct review costs stay fixed and revenue falls by €141,000; most of the retained margin disappears. The partner may still add its own markup, but the software company cannot behave like a wholesale licence with a token fee. Its defendable share must pay for analyst review, integration variance, professional liability and support.</p>
-<p>Gross margin is protected by refusing pass-through hardware. Sensors, gateways, installation and replacement are specified and invoiced by the maintenance partner; putting them through this company's books would inflate revenue, create warranty exposure and consume working capital without creating software margin. Annual subscriptions should be billed quarterly in advance, onboarding 50% in advance and 50% on acceptance. Even then, the year-one company needs roughly €60,000–€80,000 for a six-month founder gap, legal drafting, insurance, a contracted analyst and security work before two partners are live. Keep at least two months of mature payroll and contractor cost—about €70,000 under this model—rather than treating all cash as owner income.</p>
+<p>In the year-three design, the company can pay the target owner compensation and retain meaningful cash. It also reveals how little room there is for channel discounting. Cut recurring prices by 25% while direct review costs stay fixed and revenue falls by €141,000; most retained margin disappears. The partner may add its own margin, but the software company cannot behave like a wholesale licence with a token fee. Its share must pay for analyst review, integration variance, professional liability and support.</p>
+<p>The route is a hurdle, not a forecast:</p>
+<div class="ledger-wrap"><table class="ledger">
+<thead><tr><th>Gate</th><th>Paying partners / plants / sites / assets</th><th>Onboarding and recurring revenue</th><th>Staffing, churn and cash</th></tr></thead>
+<tbody>
+<tr><td><strong>Year 1</strong></td><td>Two partners, four plants, four sites and 500 active assets; two sites per partner.</td><td>Two production onboardings; €36k onboarding and €84k recurring revenue.</td><td>Founder plus contracted analyst; €75k founder replacement cost; no churn assumed; €4k operating loss.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><strong>Year 3</strong></td><td>Five partners, twelve plants, twelve sites and 1,920 assets; 2.4 sites per partner.</td><td>Four gross partner onboardings; €72k onboarding and €564k recurring revenue.</td><td>Founder, one analyst, one delivery engineer and one partner-success role. €100k owner compensation and €188k retained before tax. No partner may exceed three sites or 25% of recurring revenue. The hurdle allows at most one partner loss in 24 months and one site loss a year.</td></tr>
+<tr><td><strong>Year 5</strong></td><td>Twenty partners, forty plants, forty sites and 6,400 assets; two sites per partner.</td><td>Eight gross partner onboardings in each of years four and five, with one loss across those years. Year-five onboarding is €144k; recurring revenue reaches a €1.882m run rate.</td><td>Founder replacement, three analysts, three delivery engineers and two partner-success roles. Owner compensation remains €100k; retained cash must exceed €300k before tax after the added staff and overhead. Annual partner churn must stay below 10%, and no partner may exceed 10% of recurring revenue.</td></tr>
+</tbody></table></div>
+<p>Gross margin is protected by refusing pass-through hardware. Sensors, gateways, installation and replacement are specified and invoiced by the maintenance partner. Putting them through this company's books would inflate revenue, create warranty exposure and consume working capital without creating software margin. Annual subscriptions should be billed quarterly in advance, with onboarding 50% in advance and 50% on acceptance. Even then, the year-one company needs roughly €60,000–€80,000 for a six-month founder gap, legal drafting, insurance, a contracted analyst and security work. Keep at least two months of year-three payroll and contractor cost—about €54,000 under this design—rather than treating all cash as owner income.</p>
 <p>The fragile input is analyst time. If every alarm takes bespoke investigation and every report needs founder editing, direct cost scales with assets while price is negotiated down as “software.” The operational metric is not assets connected. It is reviewed exceptions per 1,000 asset-days, minutes per reviewed exception, percentage resolved from the runbook, and percentage of escalations with a coded work outcome. A partner that will not return outcomes should pay a higher service price or not be onboarded.</p>
 </section>
 
 <section class="scale">
 <h2>From service to company</h2>
-<p><strong>Customer one</strong> is mostly integration and vocabulary. The founder maps motor “P-04 DE” to the CMMS asset, discovers time-zone and sampling errors, watches the analyst triage alerts and writes the report with the partner. Nothing about that delivery is scalable, but each exception should produce a fixture, validation rule or runbook step.</p>
-<p><strong>Customer five</strong> should use a canonical asset schema, a versioned importer kit and one escalation taxonomy. A trained implementation engineer handles the majority of ingestion and identity work. The partner's technician resolves low-confidence measurements from a checklist. The specialist sees only a queue with operating context, prior measurements and recent work. Reports are generated from approved findings, not rewritten in prose. The founder attends the service review, not every alert.</p>
-<p><strong>Customer twenty</strong> is viable only if acquisition is through three to five enabled maintenance partners and most delivery is teachable. The recurring work is genuine: new measurements arrive, assets change duty, sensors move, thresholds need review, work closes and failure labels alter future triage. The accumulating asset is not the customer's raw waveform. It is a tested connector library, asset-identity mappings, de-identified diagnostic features where contracts permit them, alert-to-work histories, calibrated partner playbooks and evidence of false-alert and escalation performance by asset class.</p>
+<p><strong>Paying partner one</strong> starts with one end-customer plant and should reach two monitored sites. Delivery is mostly integration and vocabulary. The founder maps motor “P-04 DE” to the CMMS asset, discovers time-zone and sampling errors, watches alert triage, and writes the report with the partner. Each exception should produce a fixture, validation rule or runbook step.</p>
+<p><strong>Paying partner five</strong> should bring the total to roughly twelve plants and twelve monitored sites. Delivery uses a canonical asset schema, a versioned importer kit and one escalation taxonomy. A trained implementation engineer handles most ingestion and identity work. The partner's technician resolves low-confidence measurements from a checklist. The specialist sees a queue with operating context, prior measurements and recent work. Reports come from approved findings, not rewritten prose. The founder attends the service review, not every alert.</p>
+<p><strong>Paying partner twenty</strong>, not site twenty, is the scalability gate because the partner signs and can churn. It should bring the portfolio to about forty plants and forty sites. Partners six through twenty must arrive through a documented referral programme for sensor distributors, reliability specialists and CMMS implementers. The twentieth must have no prior founder relationship. It receives a standard data template, demonstration, diagnostic offer and production contract; bespoke founder-led courtship fails the gate.</p>
+<p>At that point, most delivery must also be teachable. Recurring work is genuine: measurements arrive, assets change duty, sensors move, thresholds need review, work closes and failure labels alter future triage. The accumulating asset is not the plant's raw waveform. It is a tested connector library, asset-identity mappings, permitted de-identified features, alert-to-work histories, calibrated partner playbooks and performance evidence by asset class.</p>
 <p>Those rights must be written before data arrives. The plant owns its raw operating data and asset history; the maintenance partner owns its service notes and commercial relationship; the software company gets a limited licence to process both for the service. Any right to retain de-identified derived features after termination is separate and explicit. The contract must specify export format, deletion, sub-processors, model-training permission, who may contact the plant, and a transition period. If the partner insists that every derived field is its confidential data and can demand a full export while prohibiting reuse, it is buying labour and hosting, not funding an accumulating software asset.</p>
 <p>Diagnostic liability needs the same precision. The service-level agreement can promise ingestion freshness, queue response and report delivery. It should not warrant detection of every fault or prescribe that a machine continue running. Each output records source quality, analyst, confidence, evidence and disposition; critical findings require partner acknowledgement; silence escalates through a named route. Professional-indemnity and cyber cover, contract caps and exclusions are necessary but not sufficient. The operational defence is the audit trail that shows what was measured, what was missing, what the layer said and who accepted the decision.</p>
 </section>
 
 <section class="competition">
 <h2>The incumbent gets a turn</h2>
-<p>Siemens can pair broad ingestion with an established predictive-maintenance workflow. <!-- CHECK: 04-senseye-ingestion --> IBM can tell an existing Maximo customer that asset health and work history belong in the system already running maintenance. <!-- CHECK: 04-maximo-bundle --> Augury can bundle sensors, diagnostics and expert validation; SKF can bundle sensors, CMMS interfaces, certified analysts and field service. <!-- CHECK: 04-augury-bundle --><!-- CHECK: 04-skf-bundle --> Their most effective response is not a price cut. It is one purchase order, one liability framework and an installed base.</p>
-<p>The channel is equally dangerous. ERIKS already describes its own Smart Asset Management platform and software team; SPIE already spans maintenance advice, implementation and an EAM partnership. <!-- CHECK: 04-eriks-offer --><!-- CHECK: 04-spie-offer --> A capable regional partner can standardise on SKF, Siemens or a CMMS module, or hire the analyst after the white-label offer proves demand. The plant may insist on its corporate APM system, making the proposed layer an exporter with no user interface. At the low end, an InfluxDB/Grafana-style stack plus PDFs may be good enough because the scarce value is the technician who knows the machine.</p>
+<p>Siemens documents broad Senseye ingestion for predictive-maintenance use. <!-- CHECK: 04-senseye-ingestion --> IBM can tell a Maximo customer that asset health and work history belong in its maintenance system. <!-- CHECK: 04-maximo-bundle --> Augury can bundle sensors, diagnostics and expert validation. <!-- CHECK: 04-augury-bundle --> SKF can bundle CMMS interfaces, certified analysts and field service. <!-- CHECK: 04-skf-bundle --> Their most effective response is not a price cut. It is one purchase order, one liability framework and an installed base.</p>
+<p>The channel is equally dangerous. ERIKS describes sensor-based Smart Asset Management and machine-fleet insight. <!-- CHECK: 04-eriks-offer --> SPIE spans maintenance advice, system implementation and an EAM partnership. <!-- CHECK: 04-spie-offer --> A capable regional partner can standardise on SKF, Siemens or a CMMS module. It can also hire the analyst after the white-label offer proves demand. The plant may insist on its corporate APM system, making the proposed layer an exporter with no user interface. At the low end, an InfluxDB/Grafana-style stack plus PDFs may be good enough. The scarce value may remain the technician who knows the machine.</p>
 <p>The entrant therefore competes on neutrality and partner economics: mixed sensors, fast onboarding of ugly route exports, the partner's brand, auditable review and no direct sale. But neutrality becomes a feature incumbents can copy. The harder defence is reciprocal dependency: the software layer makes the partner's analysts and technicians more productive, while their closed work orders improve its runbooks. If only one side learns, that side eventually captures the margin.</p>
 </section>
 
